### NOR as a primitive: intermediate-result polarity

Following Harel's request, where no explicit restriction exists, treat NOR and NAND as available gates in their own right rather than automatically decomposing them into OR/AND plus NOT. Still state fan-in and gate library when counting components or claiming optimality.

Y7=X7, Y6=X6 AND NOT(X7), N5=NOR(X6,X7), Y5=X5 AND N5 is correct. But N4=NOR(N5,X5) is not the correct continuation: N5=1 means no 1 occurs in X7,X6, whereas a cumulative OR wire would mean the opposite. Another NOR on N5 does not extend the same check.

Counterexample: X7=1,X6=0,X5=0,X4=1, completed as 10010000. N5=0, then NOR(N5,X5)=NOR(0,0)=1, so the incorrect formula also outputs Y4=1 even though X7 was already the leftmost 1.

The correct continuation preserves the meaning “all higher bits are zero”:
N6=NOT(X7);
N5=NOR(X7,X6);
N4=N5 AND NOT(X5);
N3=N4 AND NOT(X4);
N2=N3 AND NOT(X3);
N1=N2 AND NOT(X2);
N0=N1 AND NOT(X1);
Yi=Xi AND Ni for i=6..0, and Y7=X7.

Equivalently N4=NOR(NOT(N5),X5), not NOR(N5,X5). Direct wide NOR gives N4=NOR(X7,X6,X5), N3=NOR(X7,X6,X5,X4), and so on. These are multi-input gates and must explicitly be allowed; decomposition into two-input gates requires correct polarity handling. NOR cannot simply be chained as if it were OR.

Wide-NOR alternative: Y7 is a direct wire; Y6 needs NOT and AND; each of Y5..Y0 needs a suitably wide NOR and AND. If every 2..7-input NOR is one primitive cell and NOT is one cell, the count is 6 NOR+1 NOT+7 AND=14 cells. This differs from the model of 20 two-input AND/OR/NOT gates and does not imply a physical minimum or equal area per gate. Y0 is included: there are eight outputs, not seven.

Checks: the corrected cumulative condition and direct NOR models passed all 256 inputs against highest-set-bit isolation; the counterexample to the incorrect recurrence was also checked.
